'''Ohwun De Maal''' was a [[Duros]] [[Gender|male]] who worked as a freelance [[flight instructor]] and occasional [[smuggler]]. He owned and operated several [[Hangar|docking bays]] in [[Mos Eisley]] [[spaceport]] on the [[planet]] [[Tatooine]] with his [[Marriage|spouse]], [[Chachi De Maal]]. The couple often used false names when involved in shady business, with Ohwun also using the names '''Ellor Madak''' and '''Ellorrs Madak'''.

In [[1 BBY]], Ohwun and Chachi were talking in [[Chalmun's Spaceport Cantina]] in Mos Eisley when a group including [[moisture farmer]] [[Luke Skywalker]] and [[Jedi Master]] [[Obi-Wan Kenobi]] entered. The pair looked over when the newcomers got into an altercation at the bar, and not long after Skywalker and Kenobi departed Mos Eisley aboard the ''[[Millennium Falcon]]'', a [[YT-1300 light freighter]] that had been parked in [[Docking Bay 94|one]] of the De Maals' docking bays.

==Biography==
===Docking bay duo===
[[File:OhwunChachiDeMaal-SWCT.png|thumb|right|300px|The De Maals owned and operated several docking bays together.]]
Ohwun De Maal was a [[Duros]] [[Gender|male]] [[Marriage|married]] to fellow Duros [[Chachi De Maal]]. Together, the couple owned and operated a number of [[Hangar|docking bays]] in [[Mos Eisley]]<ref name="Ohwun" /> [[spaceport]] on the [[planet]] [[Tatooine]].<ref name="CL">[[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2016)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'']]</ref> He also worked as a freelance [[flight instructor]] and occasional [[smuggler]],<ref name="GentleGiant" /> [[Pilot|making]] regular deep-[[Realspace|space]] runs with Chachi that connected through Mos Eisley.<ref name="CVD">''[[Star Wars: The Complete Visual Dictionary, New Edition]]''</ref> The couple often used false names when working on shadier operations,<ref name="Ohwun" /> with Ohwun also being known as Ellor Madak<ref name="CL" /> or Ellorrs Madak<ref name="GentleGiant" /> and Chachi going by Baniss Keeg.<ref name="CL" />

In [[1 BBY]],<ref name="Date">{{FilmDate|IV|1}}</ref> [[Captain]] [[Han Solo]] and his co-pilot, [[Chewbacca]], stored their [[YT-1300 light freighter]], the ''[[Millennium Falcon]]'', in [[Docking Bay 94]],<ref name="ANH">{{Film|IV}}</ref> one of the De Maals' Mos Eisley bays.<ref name="Chachi">{{ForceCollection|Chachi De Maal|star=2}}</ref> While the [[freighter]] was parked there,<ref name="ANH" /> Ohwun and Chachi were having a heated discussion<ref name="Blog">{{SW|url=news/much-to-learn-you-still-have-9-things-you-might-not-know-about-the-aliens-of-star-wars-a-new-hope|text=''Star Wars'' at 40 &#124; Much to Learn You Still Have: 9 Things You Might Not Know About the Aliens of ''Star Wars: A New Hope''}}</ref> in [[Durese]]<ref name="Language">{{SWKidsYouTube|series=Star Wars By the Numbers|text=Every Language in Star Wars Movies|video=CZp8Civk2co|nolive=1}}</ref> while leaning against a wall<ref name="Paperback">[[Star Wars: A New Hope (paperback novel)|''Star Wars: A New Hope'' junior novelization]]</ref> in one of the booths closest to the entrance of [[Chalmun's Spaceport Cantina]] in Mos Eisley<ref name="CL" /> when the local [[moisture farmer]] [[Luke Skywalker]] entered the [[cantina]] with the [[Jedi Master]] [[Obi-Wan Kenobi]] and the [[droid]]s [[R2-D2]] and [[C-3PO]].<ref name="ANH" />

===Chaos in the cantina===
{{Quote|Greedo's gun was out its holster.|Ohwun De Maal provides a statement on Greedo's death|<ref name="Activity">''[[Star Wars: A New Hope: Activity Book]]''</ref>}}
When Skywalker and Kenobi got into an altercation with the criminals [[Doctor]] [[Cornelius Evazan]] and [[Ponda Baba]] at the bar, the two Duros looked over at the commotion<ref name="ANH" /> but soon returned to talking jive between themselves.<ref name="CL" /> Kenobi and Skywalker then hired Solo to take them to the planet [[Alderaan]], with Ohwun having moved to sit at a table with<ref name="ANH" /> the [[Givin]] smuggler [[Elis Helrot]]<ref name="CL" /> by the [[time]] Skywalker and Kenobi left the cantina.<ref name="ANH" />

[[File:DeMaahlsDistracted-ANH.png|thumb|left|300px|The De Maals witnessed a fight at the cantina.]]
Solo then shot and [[Death|killed]] the [[bounty hunter]] [[Greedo]] in a booth in front of where Ohwun was sat and left the cantina soon after. He and his passengers then had to make a [[Secret mission to Tatooine|hasty exit]] from Docking Bay 94 in the ''Falcon'' after a [[blaster]] fight with a group of [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] [[stormtrooper]]s in the docking bay.<ref name="ANH" /> Ohwun was later questioned about Greedo's death and stated that [[bounty]] [[Hunting|hunter's]] [[DT-12 heavy blaster pistol|blaster]] had been out its [[holster]] at the time of the incident,<ref name="Activity" /> which was true.<ref name="ANH" />

==Personality and traits==
Ohwun De Maal had [[Color|red]] [[eye]]s and blue-green skin.<ref name="ANH" />

==Equipment==
While in the cantina, Ohwun wore a silvery white [[flight suit]].<ref name="ANH" /> He also owned a [[blaster pistol]].<ref name="Math">''[[Star Wars Workbook: 3rd Grade Math]]''</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
===Filming and costume===
[[File:Cantina_masks_MOSW.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Duros masks were made for supplemental shots for the cantina sequence.]]
Ohwun De Maal first appeared in the [[original trilogy]] film [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|''Star Wars'': Episode IV ''A New Hope'']],<ref name="ANH" /> which was released on [[May 25]], [[1977]].<ref name="YearByYear">''[[Star Wars Year By Year: A Visual History, New Edition]]''</ref> Modelmaker [[Jon Berg]] portrayed both Ohwun and Chachi<ref name="MotE">{{SWArchive|url=fans/events/cv_masters_of_the_empire/index.html|text=Masters of the Empire}}</ref> uncredited at different points,<ref name="ANH" /> with [[Kim Falkinburg]] also portraying the character,<ref name="ShowMaster">{{WebCite|url=https://www.showmasterssales.com/collections/star-wars/products/kim-falkenberg-10x8-signed-in-blue-star-wars-a-new-hope|text=Kim Falkenberg 10X8 Signed in Blue - Star Wars A New Hope|work=[http://showmasterssales.com Showmasters Comic Con Official Autograph Shop]|archivedate=20240723082113}}</ref> during a series of supplemental shots filmed for the cantina sequence<ref name="YouTube">{{YouTube|video=d1AAd1tsPds|text=Star Wars: A New Hope - Cantina Archeology Part 2: Behind The Scenes of the Mos Eisley Cantina - US|channel=@CredibleNerds|name=The Credible Nerds}}</ref> on a sound stage in Dovington's Hollywood studio between [[January 24]] and [[January 25|25]], 1977.<ref name="MakingOf">''[[The Making of Star Wars: The Definitive Story Behind the Original Film]]''</ref>

The two characters' unarticulated, slip-on masks<ref name="Rolecall">{{SWArchive|url=episode-iv/bts/article/f20030502/index.html|text=Cantina Roll-Call: Shedding Light on Some Alien Aliases|int=Cantina Roll-Call: Shedding Light on Some Alien Aliases}}</ref> were created from a single sculpt<ref name="Moviemaking">''[[The Moviemaking Magic of Star Wars: Creatures & Aliens]]''</ref> by [[Phil Tippett]]<ref name="Rolecall" /> that was then cast twice<ref name="Moviemaking" /> in latex. The dimensions of the masks were 20 inches by 12 inches by 12 inches (51 centimeters by 31 centimeters by 31 centimeters), and they had a slit in the back for ease of use that could be sealed by a strip of Velcro on the interior. The inside of the mask's head was stuffed with foam for support. The eyes were made of plastic and finished with speckled red paint,<ref name="Auction">{{WebCite|url=https://us.propstoreauction.com/view-auctions/catalog/id/48/lot/12069/index.html|text=Lot 203 of 485: STAR WARS: EPISODE IV - A NEW HOPE - Duros Cantina Alien Mask|work=[https://propstoreauction.com/ Prop Store Auction]|archivedate=20230725211255}}</ref> with the characters becoming known as "Goggle-Eyes" on set.<ref name="Moviemaking" />

One of the two Duros masks ended up in the collection of Tippett and, at one point, had sympathetic stabilization carried out on it by [[Tom Spina]] Designs, who internally patched areas where the latex had split and created a custom support to preserve its shape. In [[2016]], the mask was in overall good condition with some minor wear and sold at auction for $55,000.<ref name="Auction" /> Only Chachi was fitted with [[alien]] hands, with Ohwun visibly having gloved human hands in the film.<ref name="Moviemaking" /> The blooper reel for ''A New Hope'' shows that on one occasion the actor playing Chachi jabbed the chest of the actor playing Ohwun too hard and broke one of the alien glove's fingers.<ref name="MakingOf" />

[[File:Apestronauts.png|thumb|left|300px|Ohwun De Maal's spacesuit was previously used in ''Escape from the Planet of the Apes''.]]
Ohwun's spacesuit costume was a studio hand-me-down<ref name="Blog" /> that was one of several that were originally made in silver for the [[1960s#1966|1966]] [[Wikipedia:Jerry Lewis|Jerry Lewis]] film [[Wikipedia:Way...Way Out|''Way&hellip;Way Out'']]. The suits were then reused in the 1967 film [[Wikipedia:In Like Flint|''In Like Flint'']] and in a two-part episode of [[Wikipedia:The Green Hornet (TV series)|''The Green Hornet'']] television series titled "Invasion from Outer Space," which aired on [[March 17]] of that year. On [[September 26]], 1969, they appeared in "Pheasant Under Glass," an episode of the [[Wikipedia:Get Smart|''Get Smart'']] television series, and then in [[1971]], they were then painted white and used in the film [[Wikipedia:Escape from the Planet of the Apes|''Escape from the Planet of the Apes'']]. Finally, before ''Star Wars'', one was used in a sketch called "Planet of the Shapes" on [[Wikipedia:The Pepsodent Show#Swan Soap and The Bob Hope Show|''The Bob Hope Show'']] on [[September 13]], 1971.<ref name="Apemania">{{WebCite|url=http://apemania.com/APE-O-NAUTS.htm|text=ESCAPE FROM THE PLANET OF THE APES APE-O-NAUT Suits|work=[http://apemania.com apemania.com]|archivedate=20230725112827}}</ref> For ''A New Hope'', Berg wore the suit with a pair of knock-off white [[Wikipedia:Adidas|Adidas]] sneakers, and two other cantina patrons, often referred to as the "spacemen," also wore the same suits. An alternate Duros costume consisting of a silver jacket, black pants, and the alien gloves was also used at one stage.<ref name="YouTube" />

===Naming===
[[File:OwhunBust-GentleGiant.png|thumb|right|300px|Gentle Giant Studios produced a bust of Ohwun De Maal.]]
In the current ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[canon]], Ohwun was first identified in the [[Star Wars video games|mobile card game]] ''[[Star Wars: Force Collection]]'',<ref name="Ohwun" /> which initially launched in [[2013]]. Although the launch of ''Force Collection'' predates<ref name="Force Collection release">{{SW|url=news/announcing-star-wars-force-collection|text=Announcing Star Wars: Force Collection|nolive=1}}</ref> the ''Star Wars'' canon reset of [[2014]],<ref name="YearByYear" /> [[Lucasfilm Story Group]] member [[Leland Chee]] confirmed that the game was kept updated to fit with canon.<ref name="Twitter">{{Twitter|holocronkeeper|status/846807700482666496|[[Leland Chee]]|date=2017-03-28|quote=I'm not gonna go through the trouble of changing them and I don't have any reason to believe currently that anyone would. [&hellip;] On the off-chance that we DID have a current need to change any of these, we would have for Force Collection.|archivedate=20210505080831|archivefile=File:Leland_Chee_Force_Collection.png|nolive=1}} &mdash; [[Leland Chee]] of the [[Lucasfilm Story Group]] has confirmed that [[in-universe]] information from ''[[Star Wars: Force Collection]]'' conforms with ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[canon]].</ref> The 2016 reference book [[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2016)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'']] then introduced the name "Ellor Madak" for the character,<ref name="CL" /> and the product page for the [[2023]] bust of the character produced by [[Gentle Giant Studios]] used the name "Ellorrs Madak."<ref name="GentleGiant" />

All three names originated in the [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] continuity,<ref name="Gamer2">{{GamerCite|2|The University of Sanbra Guide to Intelligent Life: The Duros}}</ref> where Ohwun De Maal was first mentioned in the [[1993]] [[West End Games]] ''[[Star Wars: The Roleplaying Game]]'' supplement ''[[Galaxy Guide 7: Mos Eisley]]'', which was written by [[Martin Wixted]] but did not associate the name with the Duros seen in ''A New Hope''.<ref name="GG7">''[[Galaxy Guide 7: Mos Eisley]]''</ref> A card in the [[1995]] "[[Premiere Limited]]" expansion of the [[Decipher]] ''[[Star Wars Customizable Card Game]]'' then identified the film character as "Ellorrs Madak."<ref name="CCG">{{CCG|set=Premiere Limited|cardname=Ellorrs Madak}}</ref> "[[The University of Sanbra Guide to Intelligent Life: The Duros]]," an article published in [[Wizards of the Coast]]'s ''[[Star Wars Roleplaying Game]]'' magazine [[Star Wars Gamer 2|''Star Wars Gamer'' 2]]<ref name="Gamer2" /> in [[February]] [[2001]],<ref name="Gamer 2">{{SWArchive|url=gaming/other/rpg/news20010118.html|text=''Star Wars Gamer'' #2}}</ref> established that Ohwun De Maal and Ellorrs Madak were one and the same,<ref name="Gamer2" /> with the spelling of "Ellor Madak" introduced by [[James Luceno]]'s [[2004]] reference book ''[[Inside the Worlds of Star Wars Trilogy]]''.<ref name="Inside">''[[Inside the Worlds of Star Wars Trilogy]]''</ref>

